**Current support versus accepted design.** Explicit `@project` is implemented. HTML pages currently become entries from root runtime work or fragments and use reserved `page_title`, `page_head` and related names. Root-only `$page`, explicit purposes and capability-checked `io.set_title` are accepted queued design. Keep live operational `page_*` declarations until that cutover. See @../progress (Progress) and @../progress/packages-and-builders (Packages and builders).

This page owns entry metadata and page directives. It no longer describes accepted `config:` blocks.

### `@project`

`@project` is an explicitly bound immutable namespace. The accepted `$project`
directive publishes its predefined fields only. Today's grouped record also
publishes supported extra metadata until that configuration cutover.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @project version

    footer #= [: v[version]]
]

Normal project modules and eligible project-owned support packages may bind it.
It is never implicitly injected or directly re-exported. Project-package
exposure restrictions and field provenance still apply. Config helper and input
bindings do not become project metadata merely because they exist.

See @#project-config (Project configuration) for the field contract and
@#build-inputs (Build configuration values) for independent input resolution.

### `$page`

`$page` is accepted queued HTML-builder metadata and the first planned root purpose. Bare `$page` uses the declared defaults. `$page()` is invalid.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page(
        title = "Moth",
        description = "A language for creating reliable software",
        lang = "en",
        favicon = @assets/favicon.svg,
    )
]

The positional parameter order is `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`, `body_style`, `head`. All six accept compile-time `String?` values, including structural resource-bearing Strings, and default to `none`.

Placement:

- top level of a normal module root, at most once
- the explicitly selected synthetic single-file root, at most once
- invalid in helper files, support roots, facades, `config.moth`, bodies, `export:` and a second `$page` in the same root

`$page` describes the whole root. Same-file forward references in its arguments remain invalid.

### Initial document metadata

Every parameter is `String? = none`. `none` means no page override, including
when supplied explicitly. A present empty String remains a present value where
the existing metadata domain permits it.

- A present `title` supplies the base title. Otherwise use the existing
  route-title fallback, then the project name. Apply the builder's initial title
  prefix and postfix to that base title.
- An absent `description` emits no description element. Absent `lang`,
  `favicon` and `body_style` use the HTML builder's document defaults.
- An absent `head` adds no markup. A present `head` is one folded String
  inserted as HTML with its meaningful whitespace preserved.

Escape title text and attribute values for their existing HTML contexts.
`head` is deliberately authored HTML, not title text. Compose it through normal
constant/template expressions. There are no typed head nodes, repeated-call
merges, `+=` metadata updates or last-write-wins behaviour.

These are specific document-shell fallbacks, not generic inheritance between
project and page configuration. Keep the same shell policy for HTML-JS and the
existing HTML-Wasm output path.

### Metadata ownership and resources

Arguments use ordinary root-file compile-time visibility, including earlier
same-file constants, dependency-bound constants, explicit `@project` members
and resolved source configuration values. Same-file forward references remain
invalid.

The module folds its own metadata once and stores owned non-HIR facts. Provider
metadata never becomes consumer metadata, and entry activation never compiles
it again. Resource and site-root anchors survive folding and remapping until
output planning. Retain the authored argument spans on metadata resource uses.

A tracked favicon enters the ordinary graph and resource pipeline. Only uses
reachable from selected metadata, fragments or code enter output unions.
Builder resource handling never scans rendered HTML or HIR constant names.
A metadata-only page can therefore emit its document and resources without
artificial runtime work.

Runtime title mutation is a separate, queued Core IO capability. Its exact
behaviour is defined in @../packages/core/io/ (Core IO).


### Explicit root purpose

Top-level executable work or direct output requires a recognised purpose with a consumer. Only `$page` works initially. Functions, types and constants without purpose remain a valid API-only root. Semantically classified `$doc`, `$note` or `$todo` templates do not require a purpose. Future `$test` is a later non-page purpose; it cannot satisfy validation until implemented.

### Command policy

A declaration-only root at `entry_root` is valid. HTML application `build` and `dev` need a page somewhere below it, not necessarily a homepage at `entry_root`. `check` accepts valid zero-page projects and still rejects unpurposed runtime work. Single-file HTML output requires `$page`.

### Current page metadata

Until the cutover, operational pages keep reserved names such as `page_title` and `page_head`. Do not add live `$page` statements merely because this page documents them.

